Work on the interior of the real domain: $x>0$, with $|x|>1$. For the inverse factor, the derivative formula gives $\frac{d}{dx}\operatorname{arccsc}(x)=-\frac{1}{|x|\sqrt{x^2-1}}$. Here the argument and its derivative are $u(x)=x$ and $u'(x)=1$.

Set $p(x)=\log_{4}(x)$ and $v(x)=\operatorname{arccsc}(x)$. Then $p'(x)=\frac{1}{x\ln(4)}$ and $v'(x)=-\frac{1}{|x|\sqrt{x^2-1}}$. Apply the product rule:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f'(x)&=p'(x)v(x)+p(x)v'(x)\\
&=\left(\frac{1}{x\ln(4)}\right)\cdot \left(\operatorname{arccsc}(x)\right)\\
&\quad +\left(\log_{4}(x)\right)\cdot \left(-\frac{1}{|x|\sqrt{x^2-1}}\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

Move each unchanged factor into its fraction numerator.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f'(x)&=\frac{\operatorname{arccsc}(x)}{x\ln(4)}\\
&\quad -\frac{\log_{4}(x)}{|x|\sqrt{x^2-1}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Work on the interior of the real domain: $x>0$, with $|x|>1$. For the inverse factor, the derivative formula gives $\frac{d}{dx}\operatorname{arcsec}(x)=\frac{1}{|x|\sqrt{x^2-1}}$. Here the argument and its derivative are $u(x)=x$ and $u'(x)=1$.

Set $p(x)=\ln(x)$ and $v(x)=\operatorname{arcsec}(x)$. Then $p'(x)=\frac{1}{x}$ and $v'(x)=\frac{1}{|x|\sqrt{x^2-1}}$. Apply the product rule:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f'(x)&=p'(x)v(x)+p(x)v'(x)\\
&=\left(\frac{1}{x}\right)\cdot \left(\operatorname{arcsec}(x)\right)\\
&\quad +\left(\ln(x)\right)\cdot \left(\frac{1}{|x|\sqrt{x^2-1}}\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

Move each unchanged factor into its fraction numerator.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f'(x)&=\frac{\operatorname{arcsec}(x)}{x}\\
&\quad +\frac{\ln(x)}{|x|\sqrt{x^2-1}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Work on the interior of the real domain: all real inputs. For the inverse factor, the derivative formula gives $\frac{d}{dx}\operatorname{arccot}(x)=-\frac{1}{1+x^2}$. Here the argument and its derivative are $u(x)=x$ and $u'(x)=1$.

Set $p(x)=-2x^{2}-2$ and $v(x)=\operatorname{arccot}(x)$. Then $p'(x)=-4x$ and $v'(x)=-\frac{1}{1+x^2}$. Apply the product rule:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f'(x)&=p'(x)v(x)+p(x)v'(x)\\
&=\left(-4x\right)\cdot \left(\operatorname{arccot}(x)\right)\\
&\quad +\left(-2x^{2}-2\right)\cdot \left(-\frac{1}{1+x^2}\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

Move each unchanged factor into its fraction numerator.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f'(x)&=-4x\,\operatorname{arccot}(x)\\
&\quad -\frac{-2x^{2}-2}{1+x^2}
\end{aligned}
$$

